# Shipping feature work

Do **not** automatically push, open a pull request, or merge after each change.

Keep work on the correct `feature/<area>-<short-goal>` branch (see feature-branch rule). Implement and iterate locally unless the user asks otherwise.

## Commits and push

- **Commit** only when the user asks (or when a separate user rule explicitly allows it for that request).
- **Push** only when the user asks (`git push`, “push this”, “publish the branch”, etc.).
- Never open or merge a PR unless the user asks to ship / PR / merge.

## When to suggest a PR

After a coherent chunk of work is clearly done — or the user says they are happy and ready to move on — briefly **suggest** that it is a good time to open a PR into `main`. Do not run `gh pr create` or push until they agree.

A good suggestion sounds like: “This Scale Studio pass is in good shape to PR into `main` when you want.”

Do **not** nag after every small tweak.

## If the user asks to ship / PR / merge

Then:

1. Commit pending work if needed (concise why-focused message; never commit secrets)
2. Push the feature branch (`git push -u origin HEAD`)
3. Open a PR into `main` with `gh pr create`:
   - **Title**: short and specific
   - **Body**: 2–4 bullets — what changed and why; brief test plan
4. Merge only if they asked to merge **and** it is safe (no conflicts; checks green or not required). Otherwise leave the PR open and report blockers.
5. After merge (when requested): `git checkout main && git pull`, then delete the feature branch when clean

## PR quality bar

- One feature / one concern per PR
- Title readable in the Vercel / GitHub feed
- No “WIP”, “stuff”, or vague titles
- Return the PR URL (and merge status) when you create one

## Safety

- Never force-push `main`
- Never `--no-verify` unless the user explicitly asks
- If uncommitted work spans multiple unrelated features, split or ask before shipping
